  Issue:                                                                                      Corrupt User Profile Symptoms

Note: This issue may overlap with “Apps Not Opening” because corrupted profiles can prevent applications from launching correctly.



A corrupted user profile can cause multiple issues, including:
- Applications failing to open
- Start Menu or Taskbar not responding
- Temporary profile logins
- Missing desktop or user data

Cause:
User profile files or registry entries are damaged, preventing Windows from loading the profile correctly.

Steps / Resolution:
> troubleshooting for corrupted user profiles is covered under:
Cannot Log In / Account Issues - for login and temporary profile fixes
 Apps Not Opening - for application errors caused by profile corruption

Actions include:
- Logging in with another admin account
- Backing up the affected profile from C:\Users
- Creating a new profile and restoring user data
- Checking the registry for .bak profile entries and correcting ProfileImagePath
- Verifying permissions on the new profile folder


# Corrupt User Profile Symptoms #

## Issue ##
User cannot log in properly or Windows loads a temporary profile.  
Applications may fail to open, settings are missing, or desktop icons/files are gone.  
Symptoms may include error messages like: 

- User profile cannot be loaded  
-  A Welcome: but ur using  a temporary profile

# Cause #
The user profile may be corrupted due to:  
- Failed updates or system crashes 
-  Improper shutdowns or disk errors  
- Profile registry keys being damaged or misconfigured  

# Steps / Resolution #
- Sign out and restart Windows  
- Try logging in with another user account to isolate the issue  
- Check permissions: right click user folder -> Properties -> Security -> ensure user has full control  

# 2 - if previous dosent work
- Backup user data from C:\Users\[corrupted_profile]
- Create a new user account  
- Copy essential files (Documents, Desktop, Favorites) to the new profile  
[Screenshot.png]


## Escaltion #
- Open Registry Editor -> HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList 
- Check for ".bak" entries or incorrect Profile Image Path  
- Backup the registry before making changes  
- Correct or remove ".bak" entries as needed  
> For domain accounts: escalate to IT to check Active Directory, unlock accounts, or reset profile replication  


